A||||7||Black88GT||CD changer question||||5||
Z||000000||Black88GT||12-29-2000||01:03 PM||||When my brother bought his car, it had a 12 disc Sony Cd changer with the RF thing so he could use the stock stereo.  Well he bought a new headunit and changer so the old one is mine.  The changer itself has audio outputs (red/white) on it and of course the power chord output.  My brother says I just need to connect the power chord and wha-la.  I think I need to connect the Audio outputs and the power chord.  Who is right?<P><BR>TIA<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||12-29-2000||03:12 PM||||If your head unit has connections for line-level audio in, you will need to connect the audio outputs to those audio inputs. Otherwise you'll have to use the RF modulator through the FM, like he had it. <BR>BTW... What model of Sony changer is that? I ended up "cobbling" the RF controller on my Kenwood to give me line-level output connections.<P>Raydar  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-29-2000||04:06 PM||||When I had a CD changer hooked up to a stock radio, I had to hook a box (the RF module) between the radio and antenna then supply power to the changer and tune the FM radio to a certain freq. (freq was adjustable) It was an Alpine brand CD changer. I would assume all RF style CD changers are the same.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Black88GT||12-29-2000||09:07 PM||||Well I have an afermarket Sony CD player so the RF modulator is not needed.<P>Its the Sony CDX-52, 10 disc not 12 like I originally stated.  So will I have to use the RF modulator even though I have a sony Headunit?  There are 3 preamp outputs on my headunit, could I use them (I doubt it but I figured I would ask)?<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||12-29-2000||10:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>So will I have to use the RF modulator even though I have a sony Headunit?  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just because both units are Sony it doesn't necessarily mean that the head unit can control the changer. <BR>Are there any other connectors on the head unit besides those three sets of pre-out cables (and of course the speaker and power leads)? <BR>There should be a thick cable that plugs into the changer. At least 5-6 pins in a round or rectangular connector.<BR>The other end of that cable will plug into a connector on the RF unit OR a similar connector on the head unit, if it's equipped. <BR>Here are some "gotchas" to look out for...<BR>If your head unit will control a changer, you might still need an adapter for the cable to connect.  <BR>Also, as the technology has changed, they may have changed the data protocol of the control signals. Meaning that even if the cables connect up, they may still not be able to "talk to" each other. <BR>Not meaning to discourage you, or overload you with useless info. Just telling you about the surprises that you may run into. (I <I>hate</I> surprises.)    <BR>I'm basing all of my statements on Alpine and Kenwood equipment. Sony should be similar.<P>Raydar<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||KRMFiero||12-29-2000||11:31 PM||||The is probley 3 wires for power:<BR>Positve (Red)<BR>REM (Blue), (Remote turn on)<BR>Negative (Black<P>Red goes to + on battery<BR>Black goes to - on battery<BR>REM (should be one) goes to ignition switch or if you CD-Player had a Remote turn on wire hook it up there<P><BR>next there will be low power RCA cables<BR>just plug them into your head unit if it has a input (usally they only have a output) if it only has a output you with need the RF box<P>last hopefully you have the Controller for it so the CD changer will know what to do and what song, cd....<P><BR>maybe i helped you i'm tired goodnight<BR>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<P>  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||12-30-2000||05:26 PM||||Depending on what Sony Headunit you have, it may have "CD Changer Control" on the faceplate. If it does, chances are you can use the CD changer with the HU directly, without FM modulation. Does your Sony HU have an extra plug on the back of it? Maybe labled "CD Changer or Aux Input" If it does, and if you can plug the CD Changer into it directly without useing the FM Modulation box, then do it and see if that works. If not, and your stereo DOES have the extra funny looking plug on the back, then you can probally buy an adapter from a Sony dealer that converts that plug to Left and Right line-level inputs. If you cant plug the changer into the headunit directly, then this is the way you will have to do it. Simply plug the RCA output from the Changer to the RCA input on the adapter and set the HU to Aux input and use the remote to the CD changer to contol it. If this is difficult to understand (Like so many of my explinations are) email me with any questions you might have about it. I would use the FM/RF Modulation only as a last resort. It decreases sound quality by converting it to an FM signal. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||12-30-2000||10:15 PM||||The headunit definitely can control CD changers.  Not sure about this one though.<P>I will go out tomorrow and see if there is special CD changer outputs.  I think there is but I am not sure.  I'll keep ya updated...<P><BR>thanks for the help<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
